Yes, you can achieve this by using a JIRA automation rule called "Transition Restriction." Here's how:

1. Go to your JIRA board and click on the gear icon in the top right corner.
2. Select "Board settings" and then "Workflows."
3. Click on the workflow you want to add the restriction to.
4. In the transition you want to restrict, click on the "Add" button under the "Restrictions" section.
5. Select "Custom" and then "Script" as the type of restriction.
6. Paste the following script (change "CHECKLIST" to the name of your checklist):

```groovy
import com.atlassian.plugin.webresource.ResourceUtils
import com.atlassian.jira.component.ComponentAccessor
import com.atlassian.jira.issue.Issue
import com.atlassian.jira.transition.Transition
import com.atlassian.jira.user.ApplicationUser
import java.util.List

// Get the current user
def user = ComponentAccessor.getJiraAuthenticationContext().getLoggedInUser()

// Get the issue being transitioned
def issue = transition.getOriginalIssue()

// Check if the issue has all the checklist items completed
def checklistItems = issue.getChecksumItems(CHECKLIST)
def checklistCompleted = true
checklistItems.each { item ->
    if (!item.isDone()) {
        checklistCompleted = false
        return
    }
}

// If all items are complete, allow the transition. Otherwise, prevent it.
if (checklistCompleted) {
    return [allow: true]
} else {
    return [allow: false, errorMessages: ["Please ensure that all items in the ${CHECKLIST} checklist are completed."] as String[]]
}
```

7. Save the script and test the transition restriction.

This script checks if all items in the specified checklist ("CHECKLIST") are marked as done for the issue being transitioned. If they are, the transition is allowed. If not, the transition is prevented with an error message. You can replace "CHECKLIST" with the actual name of your checklist.

