Sure! Here's a JavaScript function that will swap the positions of the first two occurrences of adjectives (as determined by regular expressions) in a given string:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  const regex1 = /\b(\w+)\s+\b(\w+)\b/g; // matches two adjacent word-boundary words
  const regex2 = /\b(\w+)\s*\b(\w+)(?:\s|$)/g; // matches last word-boundary word and any whitespace or end of string

  return sentence.replace(regex1, '$2 $1')
                 .replace(regex2, '$2 $1 ');
}

// Example usage:
const input = "Beautiful green trees are in the park.";
const output = switchAdjectives(input);
console.log(output); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park."
```

The `switchAdjectives()` function uses two regular expressions to match the first two occurrences of adjectives in a sentence. The first regex (`/\b(\w+)\s+\b(\w+)\b/g`) matches two adjacent words that both start and end with word boundaries (`\b`). The second regex (`/\b(\w+)\s*\b(\w+)(?:\s|$)/g`) is similar, but it also allows for whitespace between the matched words and at the end of the string (`(?:\s|$)`).

The function then replaces these matches with the swapped order using string replacement (`replace()`), and returns the modified string. Note that this implementation assumes that there are no more than two adjacent adjectives in a single sentence. If your use case requires handling more complex cases, you may need to modify the regular expressions or implement a more sophisticated algorithm.

