To make the circle element in this code snippet look more 3D, you can add some CSS properties that create shadows and highlights, as well as a perspective transformation. Here's an updated version of the `.circle` class:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray) drop-shadow(-15px -15px 15px gray) drop-shadow(15px -15px 15px gray) drop-shadow(-15px 15px 15px gray);
  transform: translateZ(10px); /* Add perspective */
  transition: transform 0.3s ease-in-out;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 5px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75);
  border-radius: 70%;
}

/* Adjust the size of the shadows for different devices */
@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .circle {
    filter: drop-shadow(10px 10px 10px gray) drop-shadow(-10px -10px 10px gray) drop-shadow(10px -10px 10px gray) drop-shadow(-10px 10px 10px gray);
  }
}
```

In this example, I added four drop-shadows with negative offsets to create a sense of depth, as well as a `transform: translateZ(10px)` property to move the circle slightly forward in 3D space. You may need to adjust the size and intensity of the shadows depending on the size of your screen or device.

