To fix the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in WordPress when using Next.js, follow these steps:

1. First, make sure you have installed and configured CORS Middleware for Next.js. This middleware allows cross-origin requests to your API endpoints created with Next.js.

2. In your `next.config.js` file, add the following code:

```jsx
const withCors = require('next-cors');

module.exports = withCors({
  // Allows preflight requests
  methods: ['HEAD', 'OPTIONS'],
});
```

3. In your WordPress functions.php file, add the following code to enable CORS:

```php
add_action( 'wp_headers', function() {
    header( 'Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE' );
    header( 'Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type, Authorization' );
    header( 'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true' );
} );
```

This will allow the specified HTTP methods, headers, and credentials to be sent from any origin.

4. Finally, restart your server or refresh your browser to apply the changes. Now, your Next.js app should be able to communicate with your WordPress site without any CORS errors.

